From 20ad77df0203885945a1c975857eb21d3d4589b6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 08:19:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] kanban show: read the park through the dispatcher's
 lookup; a legacy human park says it will be blocked

Follow-up to PR #1920's review nits (t_01a1d39d64436143).

1. One park lookup. `kanban_wake_summary.wakes()` re-implemented the latest
   SCHEDULED_ENTRY_EVENTS lookup and the no-event fallback that
   `kanban_continuation.pending_wake`/`missing_wake` use. New
   `kanban_continuation.park_state(conn, task_id)` -> {payload, parked_at, timed}
   is now the only reader: pending_wake, missing_wake (which takes an
   optional pre-read `park=`), `_enforce_wake_invariant` and `wakes()` all
   go through it, so the shown backstop cannot drift from the wake the
   dispatcher acts on.

2. A legacy scheduled card with no timed wake and a human block_kind
   (needs_input/capability) is moved to `blocked` by `_enforce_wake_invariant`
   on the next tick, never woken; `kanban show` still said "wakes at T".
   New `kanban_continuation.blocks_for_human(park, block_kind)` is the
   invariant's own predicate (the dispatcher now calls it too) and
   `wakes()` reports it as `blocks_for_human` (backstop_at None). The status
   line / worker_context then read "no timed wake: the dispatcher blocks it
   for a human on its next tick". The JSON `wakes` object gains the
   `blocks_for_human` bool (additive).

Display only: scheduling decisions are unchanged (the invariant's condition
`missing_wake is not None and block_kind in HUMAN_BLOCK_KINDS` is exactly
`blocks_for_human(park, block_kind)` once missing_wake is non-None).

Proof:
- tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_show_wake.py: 8 of 13 red on origin/main
  (new contract + human case), 13/13 green here. New: display backstop ==
  dispatcher wake == expected absolute time for wake_check, clock-only,
  legacy SCHEDULED_UNTIL marker and no-parking-event parks; a monkeypatched
  park_state moves display and dispatcher together; legacy human park says
  blocked and wake_due_continuations does block it; non-human legacy and
  timed human parks still say "wakes at" and are not blocked.
- Mutants (sdlc-review mutants.py, 10/10 killed): blocks_for_human
  ignoring timed / kind, phrase ignoring the flag, backstop kept for human,
  wakes() passing no block_kind, missing_wake ignoring park=, park_state
  timed always False, untimed payload dropped, no-event parked_at changed,
  invariant using `if True`.
- Related suites green: kanban continuation, timed_park, blocker_park_wake,
  sync_running_heal, wake_check, awaiting, park_producer_finished, notifier,
  notice_guarantee, kanban_show tool suites (313 tests).
